  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1241 AM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nThis Morning through Evening: Cooler with some Rain Showers\n\nA low pressure system should continue to dig south towards the \nsouthern tip of Nevada throughout the day. This feature will aid in \nproviding more active weather later on this week, particularly on \nThursday. Before that occurs, zonal flow should prevail throughout \ntoday over the Upper Midwest. At the surface, a cold front will move \nacross the area early this morning and should be east/south of the \nforecast area by sunrise. Temperatures will be much cooler today \ncompared to the last few days, with highs mainly in the upper 60s to \nlower 70s. The region should be under subsidence for most of the day \nuntil a secondary surge of mid level moisture reaches the Upper \nMississippi River valley late this afternoon into the evening. The \nbetter synoptic scale lift should stay south of the forecast area, \nbut there still remains the potential for a few rain showers or \nstorms to develop across the southern portions of the area, mainly \nsouth of I-90. Strong capping will be in place so any showers or \nstorms that form should be elevated. Mid level CAPE may be \nsufficient enough to develop some lightning and perhaps a few small \nhailstones, but otherwise minimal impacts are forecast with any \nshower or storms that develops today.\n\nThursday: Increasing Potential for Rain Showers\n\nCooler air will continue to filter in over the Northern Plains into \nthe Midwest through the rest of the week with afternoon highs \ndecreasing a few degrees each day. The aforementioned upper level \nlow pressure system is forecast to become cutoff and should \neventually lift northeast on Thursday. The cutoff low should become \nabsorbed into a stronger trough that digs south out of Alberta and \nSaskatchewan on Thursday. As synoptic lift provided by the systems \nmoves over the Midwest, plentiful moisture should be in place to \ngenerate precipitation throughout the day. Mainly light rain amounts \nare likely with this system, however, the latest 13/01z NBM does \nhave higher probabilities for a half inch of rain (up to 30-50%) \nacross the southern half of the forecast area compared to 24 hours \nago. A cold front moving in from the northwest will quickly put an \nend to any precipitation on Thursday evening as cooler and drier air \nmoves in in its wake.\n\nFriday Night: Cold Temperatures Leading to Frost/Freeze\n\nBroad surface high builds in the wake of the Thursday night's upper \ntrough. Clearing skies and light winds after a period of post-\nfrontal CAA will very likely (70-100%) lead to widespread frost, \npotentially (30%+) a freeze and, for areas north of I-94, a chance \n(20-50%) for a killing freeze with temperatures below 28. With some \nguidance suggesting some surface pressure gradient and thus light \nwinds will be maintained Friday night will need to continue to \nmonitor this period as there is still some uncertainty with regards \nto temperatures ending up a few degrees warmer than currently \nforecast.",
